{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789502115,"nonce":"tZlZ9DnC+Ra3+L+blsCfq06UK94=","title":"fannkuch: slice assignment from a slice of the same list allocates twice and walks three times","message":"The flip loop is `perm[:k+1] = perm[k::-1]`: `zb_list_slice_i64` builds the reversed slice by push (a growth check per element), then `zb_list_assign_slice_i64` copies it again (its defence against the source aliasing the target) and assigns element by element with a bounds check each. Measured on the M1 Pro at n=9: 139 ms with direct method calls, 47 ms with the reversal written as an in-place swap loop (which is faster than PyPy's 49 ms on that variant), so this is about 92 ms of the kernel's 225 and the whole of the gap to PyPy.\n\nFixes, in order of reach: (1) `assign_slice` compares the two headers and copies the source only when they alias; (2) a slice with a known step is allocated at its final length and filled, not pushed; (3) the frontend recognises `xs[a:b] = xs[c::-1]` over the same name with matching bounds as a reverse in place (`zb_list_reverse_range`), the classic being this kernel and every permutation routine like it.","files":null}]}